# Profile entries for odrl:language over the BCP 47 slice.
#
# Two bindings over one resource, differing in how a policy value is
# resolved to a concept.  
# The sort is nom, and language is where that does visible work.  A
# registry publishes a set of subtags and, over this slice, no relation
# between them, so the resource file carries fifteen concept statements
# and no order statement at all.  There is nothing for isA to read, and
# the signature rejects isA, isPartOf and hasPart on this operand before
# the resource is opened.  The only other nominal operand in the suite is
# fileFormat; every other operand is tax or mer.
#
# The distinctness rule is a declaration and lives in its own file.  A
# problem citing a binding below without also including BCP47001-0.ax
# gets Unknown where the declaration would have given a definite verdict:
# the registry lists both subtags and says nothing that separates them.

@prefix odrl:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/odrl/2/> .
@prefix bind:    <https://w3id.org/odrl-kb/binding#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix ex:      <https://w3id.org/odrl-kb/profile/> .

# --- the grounding rules ---------------------------------------------
#
# Named, so that a binding cites one and a report says which was applied.
# A rule is a statement about how values are read, not about what the
# registry contains, and two readings of one registry by different
# rules disagree about which policies are interpretable rather than about
# what the languages are.

ex:g-bcp47-exact a bind:GroundingProcedure ;
    dcterms:title "Exact subtag match"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A right-operand value resolves to a concept of the scheme when it
         is that concept's IRI, or when it is a literal equal to the
         concept's skos:notation. Nothing else resolves. A composed tag
         such as en-US is well formed under RFC 5646 and resolves to no
         concept here, because the scheme holds primary subtags and
         en-US is not one."""@en ;
    bind:matchesProperty skos:notation ;
    bind:acceptsIRI true ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5646#section-2.2> .

ex:g-bcp47-primary a bind:GroundingProcedure ;
    dcterms:title "Primary subtag reduction"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """As the exact rule, and additionally: a value that is a
         well-formed language tag resolves to the concept whose notation
         is the tag's primary language subtag. RFC 5646 section 2.2.1
         defines the primary subtag as the first component, so en-US
         resolves to en and de-DE to de.

         This is a decision about reading, not about meaning. Adopting it
         treats a value for American English as a value for English, which
         is right for some purposes and wrong for others; declining the
         rule for the sake of the region gets an ungrounded value instead
         of a wrong answer. The profile is where that choice is recorded."""@en ;
    bind:matchesProperty skos:notation ;
    bind:acceptsIRI true ;
    bind:reducesToPrimarySubtag true ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5646#section-2.2.1> .

# --- the bindings -----------------------------------------------------

ex:b-language-bcp47 a bind:OperandBinding ;
    dcterms:title "Language over BCP 47, exact subtags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The default binding. A policy naming a subtag of the slice is
         interpretable; one naming anything else is not, and the case is
         rejected as ungrounded, with that value named in the report,
         rather than a guess at what was meant."""@en ;
    bind:leftOperand      odrl:language ;
    bind:sort             bind:nom ;
    bind:resource         <https://w3id.org/odrl-kb/bcp47> ;
    bind:grounding        ex:g-bcp47-exact .

ex:b-language-bcp47-primary a bind:OperandBinding ;
    dcterms:title "Language over BCP 47, composed tags reduced"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The same resource, the same sort, the same declarations, read by a
         wider rule. A policy that the default binding cannot interpret
         may be interpretable here, and the two bindings can therefore
         return different verdicts on one pair of policies without
         disagreeing about any concept.

         That difference is the profile's, and a report citing this
         binding says so."""@en ;
    bind:leftOperand      odrl:language ;
    bind:sort             bind:nom ;
    bind:resource         <https://w3id.org/odrl-kb/bcp47> ;
    bind:grounding        ex:g-bcp47-primary .
